1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,420
October 19, 2014. A sunspot big enough to swallow 12

2
00:00:05,420 --> 00:00:08,960
Earths crosses the Sun. And from 93 million miles away,

3
00:00:09,340 --> 00:00:10,880
we watch our star visibly dim.

4
00:00:12,260 --> 00:00:16,400
A decade later, one of the best -selling science fiction novels in years would build

5
00:00:16,400 --> 00:00:19,620
its entire plot around something far stranger doing that on purpose.

6
00:00:20,160 --> 00:00:22,520
A living thing, riding the light of a star,

7
00:00:23,180 --> 00:00:24,420
slowly starving it to death.

8
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:27,680
The book is called Project Hail Mary.

9
00:00:28,200 --> 00:00:31,160
In 2026, it became a major motion picture,

10
00:00:31,780 --> 00:00:33,580
and it carries an unusual reputation.

11
00:00:34,640 --> 00:00:36,200
Fans and critics both call it hard,

12
00:00:36,580 --> 00:00:38,640
science fiction, the kind that shows its work.

13
00:00:40,060 --> 00:00:43,360
So we did the version nobody on the movie's press tour had time for.

14
00:00:43,920 --> 00:00:46,440
We took the book's three biggest scientific claims.

15
00:00:46,800 --> 00:00:48,480
A microbe that can starve a star,

16
00:00:48,940 --> 00:00:51,820
a ship that flies at 94 % of the speed of light,

17
00:00:52,080 --> 00:00:54,500
and an alien built from a different chemistry than us,

18
00:00:54,860 --> 00:00:56,780
and checked every one of them against real,

19
00:00:56,920 --> 00:01:00,460
published science. You're watching Mindwired.

20
00:01:02,160 --> 00:01:04,500
This is the real science behind Project Hail Mary.

21
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Which of its ideas are grounded in real,

22
00:01:07,380 --> 00:01:10,860
published research? Which ones bend the rules of physics on purpose?

23
00:01:11,380 --> 00:01:15,720
And the one honest admission its own author makes about exactly where fiction takes over.

24
00:01:19,960 --> 00:01:23,140
In the story, it's called Astrophage.

25
00:01:23,540 --> 00:01:26,060
A microscopic organism that lives inside stars,

26
00:01:26,340 --> 00:01:27,780
feeds directly on their energy,

27
00:01:28,140 --> 00:01:29,640
and multiplies by the trillion,

28
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:31,680
dimming its host star as it spreads.

29
00:01:33,300 --> 00:01:36,040
So we start with the real record for extreme life on Earth.

30
00:01:36,700 --> 00:01:41,540
The hardiest organism science has ever confirmed keeps reproducing at over 250 degrees

31
00:01:41,540 --> 00:01:45,220
Fahrenheit, found thriving near hydrothermal vents on the ocean floor,

32
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:48,460
running entirely on chemistry with no sunlight at all.

33
00:01:49,920 --> 00:01:51,580
Radiation is a different real record.

34
00:01:52,080 --> 00:01:56,880
A bacterium called Dinococcus radiodurans survives radiation doses roughly a thousand

35
00:01:56,880 --> 00:02:00,220
times what would kill a human and keeps its DNA intact.

36
00:02:01,740 --> 00:02:02,740
Then there's the tardigrade.

37
00:02:03,240 --> 00:02:05,520
In a real European space agency experiment,

38
00:02:05,940 --> 00:02:09,180
it survived 10 days completely exposed to the vacuum of space.

39
00:02:09,480 --> 00:02:12,540
No suit, no shielding, direct solar radiation,

40
00:02:12,940 --> 00:02:14,040
and it didn't just survive.

41
00:02:14,600 --> 00:02:19,760
It went on to reproduce. None of that gets an organism anywhere close to living inside

42
00:02:19,760 --> 00:02:23,900
a star. The surface of our sun is almost 10 ,000 degrees Fahrenheit.

43
00:02:24,500 --> 00:02:25,900
Its core runs into the millions.

44
00:02:26,640 --> 00:02:28,400
At those temperatures matter isn't solid,

45
00:02:28,680 --> 00:02:30,440
or even a gas. It's plasma.

46
00:02:31,160 --> 00:02:33,820
Atoms torn apart into bare nuclei and free electrons.

47
00:02:34,280 --> 00:02:36,540
There is no DNA, no cell membrane,

48
00:02:36,920 --> 00:02:38,980
no chemistry of any kind that survives that.

49
00:02:40,500 --> 00:02:43,640
Real stars really do dim in ways scientists can't fully explain.

50
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:46,580
One of them, nicknamed Boyajian's star,

51
00:02:47,040 --> 00:02:49,580
dipped in brightness by as much as 22%,

52
00:02:49,580 --> 00:02:53,920
unexplained enough that astronomers seriously floated the idea of an alien megastructure

53
00:02:53,920 --> 00:02:56,460
before settling on a far less exciting answer,

54
00:02:57,020 --> 00:02:59,660
probably just an unusually large amount of orbiting dust.

55
00:03:00,960 --> 00:03:02,600
Physicist Jacqueline McCleary,

56
00:03:02,820 --> 00:03:04,180
at Northeastern University,

57
00:03:04,620 --> 00:03:06,520
ran the numbers on Astrophage specifically.

58
00:03:07,320 --> 00:03:09,040
Her conclusion, there are,

59
00:03:09,320 --> 00:03:14,380
in her words, orders of magnitude between what a microbe could ever store and what our

60
00:03:14,380 --> 00:03:17,560
sun actually radiates every second into space.

61
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:21,700
Here's the part that actually earns this book its reputation.

62
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:24,400
Its own author doesn't dispute any of this.

63
00:03:25,240 --> 00:03:26,940
Andy Weir has said, on the record,

64
00:03:27,360 --> 00:03:30,340
that Astrophage required exactly one invented rule,

65
00:03:30,760 --> 00:03:35,080
a fictional quirk in its cell membrane that lets it store energy no real biology could.

66
00:03:35,420 --> 00:03:39,200
In his words, he looked for the absolute minimum way he could break the rules,

67
00:03:39,580 --> 00:03:43,140
and then built everything else on top of real biology and real physics.

68
00:03:45,740 --> 00:03:50,920
The ship in the story is called the Hail Mary.

69
00:03:51,540 --> 00:03:53,880
Its fuel is the same organism from chapter one,

70
00:03:54,540 --> 00:03:56,360
burned for propulsion, and it's fast,

71
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:00,140
capable of reaching roughly 94 % of the speed of light.

72
00:04:01,660 --> 00:04:04,320
At that speed, time dilation isn't optional,

73
00:04:04,700 --> 00:04:06,440
it's real, measured physics.

74
00:04:07,380 --> 00:04:11,040
Clocks moving that fast really do run slower relative to the rest of the universe.

75
00:04:11,880 --> 00:04:16,320
It's been confirmed in particle accelerators and in GPS satellites correcting for it

76
00:04:16,320 --> 00:04:21,480
every single day. Oxford astrophysicist Becky Smethurst checked the

77
00:04:21,480 --> 00:04:24,900
specific numbers this book uses for its characters aging differently than the people

78
00:04:24,900 --> 00:04:26,360
back on Earth. Her verdict,

79
00:04:26,780 --> 00:04:28,120
the timelines fully check out.

80
00:04:29,560 --> 00:04:30,440
So does the acceleration.

81
00:04:31,200 --> 00:04:32,860
The ship cruises at roughly 1g,

82
00:04:33,260 --> 00:04:34,920
the same pull as Earth's gravity.

83
00:04:35,740 --> 00:04:36,520
That's not a coincidence.

84
00:04:37,100 --> 00:04:40,080
It's the number real physicists already use as the comfortable,

85
00:04:40,500 --> 00:04:44,020
survivable long -haul profile for a hypothetical relativistic voyage,

86
00:04:44,340 --> 00:04:46,320
doubling as artificial gravity for the crew.

87
00:04:47,920 --> 00:04:52,360
Getting to 94 % of light speed at all is where real engineering falls far behind.

88
00:04:53,040 --> 00:04:57,540
Humanity's actual, seriously studied interstellar concepts don't come close.

89
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:02,960
A laser -driven sail aims for 20 % of light speed and only for a probe lighter than a

90
00:05:02,960 --> 00:05:07,240
paperclip. A 1950s nuclear pulse design aimed for 10%.

91
00:05:07,240 --> 00:05:08,760
None of them ever flew a crew.

92
00:05:10,180 --> 00:05:14,000
The one real mechanism with anywhere near the needed energy density is antimatter.

93
00:05:14,620 --> 00:05:16,980
NASA has funded real feasibility studies on it.

94
00:05:17,280 --> 00:05:21,820
The problem is cost. Producing even a single gram is estimated to need costs to drop

95
00:05:21,820 --> 00:05:24,320
10 ,000 times over from where they stand today,

96
00:05:24,580 --> 00:05:28,440
and we've only ever contained trace amounts for minutes at a lab in Europe.

97
00:05:29,960 --> 00:05:33,280
Physicist Chad Orzel points out the specific problem with the ship's fuel.

98
00:05:33,900 --> 00:05:37,660
Converting mass directly into energy at anywhere near total efficiency is,

99
00:05:38,080 --> 00:05:41,780
in real physics, something only matter colliding with antimatter can do.

100
00:05:42,280 --> 00:05:46,380
He notes that even the book's own author has called his fuel organism's exact energy

101
00:05:46,380 --> 00:05:50,960
trick hand -wavy. There's also a hazard real physics can't ignore,

102
00:05:51,200 --> 00:05:52,720
that the story simply writes around.

103
00:05:53,100 --> 00:05:57,720
At those speeds, ordinary interstellar hydrogen turns into lethal radiation on impact.

104
00:05:58,700 --> 00:06:02,680
One physics paper puts the real safe ceiling for an unshielded ship at around half the

105
00:06:02,680 --> 00:06:05,760
speed of light, well under what this story's ship survives unscathed.

106
00:06:06,180 --> 00:06:08,900
Asked directly whether a real mission like this is possible,

107
00:06:09,420 --> 00:06:11,760
NASA's Lisa Carnell gave a one -line answer.

108
00:06:12,260 --> 00:06:15,360
Not right now, with any technology that currently exists.

109
00:06:16,580 --> 00:06:20,380
Weir himself has said this book took more research than anything he'd written before,

110
00:06:20,860 --> 00:06:22,340
across physics, relativity,

111
00:06:22,820 --> 00:06:27,000
and material science, and that he ran all the numbers on the fuel's energy budget himself.

112
00:06:27,480 --> 00:06:31,180
His own framing, in an interview with astrophysicist Neil deGrasse Tyson,

113
00:06:31,540 --> 00:06:33,680
is that the fuel is his one deliberate cheat,

114
00:06:34,080 --> 00:06:36,640
chosen specifically so that everything built on top of it,

115
00:06:36,860 --> 00:06:38,980
the acceleration, the time dilation,

116
00:06:39,400 --> 00:06:41,600
the flight plan, could be calculated for real.

117
00:06:42,780 --> 00:06:46,080
Chapter 3. The Friend That Shouldn't Be Possible.

118
00:06:47,840 --> 00:06:49,840
The story's most beloved character isn't human.

119
00:06:51,080 --> 00:06:53,320
Rocky is built from something closer to rock than flesh,

120
00:06:53,780 --> 00:06:56,540
evolved on a world crushed under enormous pressure,

121
00:06:57,080 --> 00:06:59,980
scorching hot with a poisonous atmosphere by our standards.

122
00:07:01,620 --> 00:07:03,360
That's not as far -fetched as it sounds.

123
00:07:03,980 --> 00:07:08,140
Real extremophiles on Earth already survive pressures over a thousand times sea level

124
00:07:08,140 --> 00:07:12,720
at hydrothermal vents that run entirely on chemistry with no sunlight at all.

125
00:07:14,380 --> 00:07:17,160
Assuming life has to look like us even has a real name in science.

126
00:07:17,820 --> 00:07:20,920
Carbon chauvinism, a term coined by Carl Sagan himself,

127
00:07:21,380 --> 00:07:23,620
who admitted even he was probably guilty of it.

128
00:07:24,400 --> 00:07:25,560
Astrobiologist Mike Wong,

129
00:07:25,800 --> 00:07:27,420
who studies exactly this question,

130
00:07:27,800 --> 00:07:31,920
says the real diversity of possible life in the universe is almost certainly wider than

131
00:07:31,920 --> 00:07:34,820
the one narrow example evolution happened to produce here on Earth.

132
00:07:36,320 --> 00:07:39,120
The film's marketing calls Rocky's chemistry ammonia -based.

133
00:07:39,780 --> 00:07:41,320
That's a slight oversimplification.

134
00:07:42,120 --> 00:07:44,520
The real story, by the author's own account,

135
00:07:44,780 --> 00:07:47,980
is a water -based creature that survives on a world with a thick,

136
00:07:48,660 --> 00:07:49,780
ammonia -heavy atmosphere,

137
00:07:50,220 --> 00:07:51,780
not a completely different biochemistry.

138
00:07:52,400 --> 00:07:55,940
The idea of life actually built on ammonia instead of water is real too,

139
00:07:56,260 --> 00:07:58,980
a 1954 hypothesis still unproven,

140
00:07:59,380 --> 00:08:00,900
usually discussed for a moon like Titan.

141
00:08:02,120 --> 00:08:05,000
The part real experts push back on hardest isn't the biology.

142
00:08:05,680 --> 00:08:07,620
It's the language. In the story,

143
00:08:08,020 --> 00:08:09,920
two minds with zero shared biology,

144
00:08:10,360 --> 00:08:14,480
culture, or reference point build a working shared language astonishingly fast.

145
00:08:15,040 --> 00:08:19,440
Linguist Hannah Little at the University of Liverpool points out that even translating

146
00:08:19,440 --> 00:08:24,240
between two human languages takes an enormous amount of data and working out which alien

147
00:08:24,240 --> 00:08:26,000
sounds even carry meaning would be,

148
00:08:26,360 --> 00:08:28,400
in her words, a mammoth task.

149
00:08:29,800 --> 00:08:34,120
The one part of that bridge real scientists do take seriously is starting with math and

150
00:08:34,120 --> 00:08:38,160
physics, the closest thing to a universal language two different minds might actually

151
00:08:38,160 --> 00:08:43,320
share. It's the exact principle behind the real interstellar message humanity beamed

152
00:08:43,320 --> 00:08:46,460
into space from the Arecibo telescope back in 1974.

153
00:08:48,180 --> 00:08:50,700
What is real is how carefully the character was built.

154
00:08:51,520 --> 00:08:53,800
Andy Weir worked directly with an astrobiologist,

155
00:08:54,260 --> 00:08:55,780
picked an actual candidate planet,

156
00:08:56,240 --> 00:08:58,740
calculated what pressure and light it would really have,

157
00:08:59,140 --> 00:09:02,220
and reverse engineered a creature that could plausibly survive there,

158
00:09:02,620 --> 00:09:06,480
down to a specific, reasoned explanation for how it produces sound at all.

159
00:09:08,020 --> 00:09:11,700
Even the planet itself is a small lesson in how fast real science moves.

160
00:09:12,580 --> 00:09:17,240
The exact real star system the author picked as a candidate home for Rocky's world has,

161
00:09:17,600 --> 00:09:21,160
since publication, been partly revised by newer observations.

162
00:09:21,920 --> 00:09:24,600
Fiction had to guess. Astronomy kept going.

163
00:09:26,080 --> 00:09:31,300
Chapter 4. So, one of the most careful science

164
00:09:31,300 --> 00:09:34,420
outlets in the world, scientific American in the New York Times,

165
00:09:34,780 --> 00:09:39,240
independently checked this entire book against real physics and biology after the movie's

166
00:09:39,240 --> 00:09:42,000
release. They landed in almost the same place.

167
00:09:43,440 --> 00:09:45,460
Their verdict, the orbital mechanics,

168
00:09:45,940 --> 00:09:49,560
the ship's flight plan, and the alien's biology are treated fairly,

169
00:09:50,060 --> 00:09:53,460
reasoned out from real constraints about as rigorously as fiction gets.

170
00:09:54,120 --> 00:09:58,220
The one place the numbers genuinely don't work is the very premise the whole story is

171
00:09:58,220 --> 00:10:01,240
built on, a microbe with enough energy to dim a star.

172
00:10:02,680 --> 00:10:05,240
Which tracks with something the author has said about his own method.

173
00:10:05,700 --> 00:10:08,400
Find the smallest possible place to break a rule of physics,

174
00:10:08,780 --> 00:10:11,420
and then respect every other rule as strictly as you can.

175
00:10:12,140 --> 00:10:13,660
Astrophage is that one break.

176
00:10:14,300 --> 00:10:16,700
Almost everything downstream of it is real science,

177
00:10:17,160 --> 00:10:18,920
pushed about as far as it can honestly go.

178
00:10:20,300 --> 00:10:22,460
Here's what actually stayed with me researching this.

179
00:10:23,020 --> 00:10:24,880
It's not that the book gets the science right,

180
00:10:25,200 --> 00:10:29,560
it's how much real, published research it takes to even know exactly where it's wrong.

181
00:10:31,020 --> 00:10:34,040
A microbe that lives inside a star will probably always be fiction,

182
00:10:34,520 --> 00:10:37,020
but the extremophiles it's loosely based on are real,

183
00:10:37,480 --> 00:10:40,400
and we're still finding stranger ones every year in trenches,

184
00:10:40,900 --> 00:10:44,180
in ice, in radiation that would kill anything else we know of.

185
00:10:45,420 --> 00:10:48,020
The gap between this story and reality isn't a flaw,

186
00:10:48,440 --> 00:10:51,860
it's a map. It shows you exactly where the real science ends,

187
00:10:52,140 --> 00:10:54,240
and points at what's actually left to go discover.

188
00:10:55,820 --> 00:10:58,620
If a fictional microbe that can starve a star got under your skin,

189
00:10:59,300 --> 00:11:00,420
wait until you see the real,

190
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:03,300
75 year old scientific argument for why,

191
00:11:03,620 --> 00:11:05,820
if alien life like Rocky's is really out there,

192
00:11:06,200 --> 00:11:07,960
we still haven't heard a single word from it.

193
00:11:08,520 --> 00:11:10,880
That full story is live on this channel right now.

